A||N||3||bHooper||how do yoiu find exhaust leaks?||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-24-2000||03:21 PM||||I just talked the Jerks at Goodyear... boy they just havent seemed to happy about having the opportunity of working on my car!<P>First off, the car is not going to pass inspection... there is an exhaust leak!  Anyone know how someone such as myself can find these?  I will need to fix them ASAP!<P>2nd) They claim that the loose belt/low voltage that I was getting last night is a frayed belt!  I just replaced it, the altenator, and the battery!  The car has squeeled twice, and now the belt is frayed?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  Loose, yes.  Frayed ?<P>3rdly) When I asked about the 4 wheel alignment ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) they played kinda dumb... "sir are you sure you want us to do that?  You know that the camber, toe in and caster is severely off on all 4 corner's, sir?"  Hell Yes!  That's why I brought it there and asked how much it was for a 4 wheel alignment... $59.95  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I dropet the damn engine and suspension... of course the alignment is bad!!<P>Needless to say, once I get my car aligned and inspected, I will never take it back to these jerks again!  They even had the audacity (sp) to question as to why I should be telling them what is wrong with my car!  I know I am no mechanic... <B>but I have brought this car back from the dead!!</B><P>sorry to rant and rave... does anyone know how to find a exauhst leak?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||Frank R||05-24-2000||04:54 PM||||Exhaust leaks are easy to find.  Raise car on jack stands.  Have friend or helper sit in car and start same.  You need to slide under car while engine is running.  Friend/helper places wet rags over tailpipe end tips.  Exhaust will make loud flatuating sounds.  This test works best when engine is warm.  Do not linger under car.  Find leaks and get out from under car.  Stop engine and say yesssssss........Frank R...||||reg||6
Z||000002||Ozzy||05-24-2000||05:27 PM||||Another way is to take a piece of tubing at least 3/8" ID. and about 3' long. Use it like a stethescope. Listen in one end while hunting around the obvious joints, gaskets and manifolds with the other. You will hear the leak easily. If it's a V-6, odds are the front manifold or x-over pipe. I just placed a second front manifold on mine and it's cracked already. Kinda sounds like a spitfire!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ozzy<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 05-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||05-24-2000||10:01 PM||||I usually go after exhaust leaks when the engine(and exhaust) are cold,then the leaks can be found quickly using your hands.<BR>||||reg||1
